on  and so again this automated platform  allowed us to expand our testing  throughout the state of kansas and so  this is you know the red dots are just  showing higher concentrations and we  expanded this testing for 95 counties in  the state of kansas  and one of the important things that  also let us do is that this testing when  we first started was mostly quarterly  and i'll show that uh here in a few  moments that if you really want to  detect these trends you've got to be  much more frequent than that and so the  automated platform allowed us to move to  at least bi-weekly testing if not more  frequent  and to kind of show you what that data  looks like i'll highlight six  communities that we're evaluating  so a wastewater facility in johnson  county atchison kansas concordia hayes  colby and  wichita so these are all again  municipalities in kansas and so when  we've moved to the more to that high  frequency testing you can start to see  these trends and that  n1 sars cov2 concentration in these  samples and again you need that kind of  more high resolution that kind of weekly  or bi-weekly sampling to catch these  spikes and that n1 concentration that  you observe and all of these communities  whereas if we were still just testing  quarterly we'd miss that  we also started and we're able to start  uh sequencing and so we are doing  sequencing now with arctic version three  primers 75 76 alternative and 77  and we're running my seek pooled three  month sample runs  and  we started this in 2021 and so some of  our first data here is for october  november december 2021 and for the most  part when you look at october in kansas  we're largely seeing you know these  kind of what i refer to as these legacy  variants so covet 19 some of the  variants that were introduced in  2020  in november we start to see or detect  delta and then really in december of  2021 is where we start to see  that omicron pop up in this particular  lawrence community  if we look at all 