y again leverage  any networks that already integrate with  operators in your  in this case in the united states states  and so we have state-based  professional societies we were also able  to leverage to help connect us to the  municipalities for this effort  and then also  kind of a two-part understanding the  limitations of wastewater surveillance  so it took a lot of work for us to be  able to take our wastewater  concentrations that we were measuring in  one community and connect those to the  case data and so that took a lot of work  but then also bringing on those people  so again the local county health  departments in our case  bringing those people on board to be  willing to give us the data to help us  interpret the data on the case data side  because you know i think we tend to  focus that there's a lot of uncertainty  on the wastewater side but i think  there's also a lot of uncertainty on the  clinical testing side of things and  especially now that we enter the stage  of the pandemic where  not all but a lot of testing in the  united states or people testing at home  and they don't always report if they  have a positive case or not  thank you for that question  great thank you  next question here what is next for  kansas for wastewater testing  it's uh always an interesting question  so we've been working on kind of one  year timelines and our timeline uh for  this round of testing is coming up in  june 30th and so we're  still trying to figure out how we're and  and who is going to do this but i think  the uh  important and kind of cool thing about  this wastewater based epidemiology  efforts is this idea that this is really  kind of a very powerful community health  tool  that a lot of health departments are  using now in their toolbox and so there  has been  a wide variety of interest across the  state to continue these efforts  and again that june 30th there may be  some transition  at the university side of things  we just have uh  an nsf project launching called adaptive  and resilient infrastructures driven by  social equity and so a lot of these  concepts  will be used in  assuring that collection systems  especially in under  invested communities are updated so that  um you know those wastewater collection  systems  can cause leakages for example into  other communities or into drinking water  systems so these are combined sewer  overflows and sanitary sewer overflows i  should pause  and back up a sec  so because we have aged infrastructure  in a lot of communities when it rains or  uh even just when there are increased  amounts of sewage  flow in a collection system this can  sometimes overload the treatment systems  and there's these raw sewage discharges  these raw sewage discharges can affect  communities both locally if you had an  infrastructure breakage within the  community or in downstream communities  for example on the kansas river where an  upstream community releases and then it  impacts the downstream and so a lot of  these uh again expanding beyond sars  kovi ii a lot of these tools are usable  in other infectious disease and so we'll  be using these tools  to both  monitor and then also suggest  improvements in infrastructure systems  across the state of kansas  great thank you  next question how effective were the  tests and were or will those results be  available to the community and where  would we be able to find these results  online  so uh we did publish at least the case  theta set um in fem's article i think  the slides are going to be released  after this presentation and so there's a  citation for  that has my last name hutchison and so  uh the lawrence case data uh is already  out there on the internet so if you can  just you know search google hutcheson  sarsko v2 uh that article will probably  pop up  and um  all of the corresponding underlying data  is available on mendeley data so we  we in addition to the publication we  shared all of our raw data as a data  publication  another question here what type of  analyzer are you using  i'm sorry could you repeat that question  yes what type of analyzer are you using  so um this uh is a multi-layered  question i think it depends on  which specific aspect of the testing  that you're asking about so for the  concentration and rna extraction we're  now using the series nanoscience  nanotrap beads  particles  the magmax rna extraction and the  kingfisher platform and then that  transitions once you start doing the  quantification the bio-rad  droplet digital pcr  system and then for sequencing we're  using the my seek run  great thank you  your next question here asks can you  list your lod again  yes um  i write these down because i am very  particularly bad at memorizing this so  the n1 quantitative limit of detection  was 1.28 gene copies per milliliter  wastewater and for n2 it's 1.1 gene  copies per milliliter of wastewater  great we have time for a few more  questions here this next one asks what  are your methods for normalizing for  creatinine and mitochondria  so for creatinine we used an elisa test  so this is just a colorimetric  intensity test based on the  concentration of creatinine in the  sample and then for mitochondria dna we  developed a qpcr assay and the primers  and again all of that information uh is  also located in the publication  thank you  next question  when using the liquid phase only for  nanotrap beads do you pre-process the  sample in any way in an effort to  release additional virus particles from  the solid phase first  so the only pre-processing step that we  have right now is that enhancement  reagent one so we do add that  which again  in our our assessment showed an  increased recovery of our mhv and also  uh across the majority of our uh  participating municipalities showed an  increase in the n1  concentrations our ability to detect the  n1 concentrations in our samples  with the transition from the platform we  stopped uh pre-centrifugation so we are  also now including the solids  in our wastewater samples so again  there's kind of a transition point  between objective one and two and  objective three so in objective one and  two we did pre-centrifuge to separate  our solids for the peg method but then  after and once we transitioned to the  other platform we stop doing the  pre-centrifugation step  great  well looks like we have time for one  more question so we'll go ahead and wrap  up with this one and it asks for your  creatinine etc correlations you use your  raw gene copies of virus per volume of  sewage cdc and majority of dashboards  are normalizing the end concentrations  to the total daily influent flow and  then sewer shed population do you think  this is the correct data to use for  those normalization correlations with  case data then  so that is an excellent question and  you're not going to like me because i'm  not going to say there's a right way yet  i think there's a lot of work out there  trying to figure this out um  we  generally still report concentration  data for most of our municipalities the  reason why we do concentration data when  we're just directly reporting to  municipalities some of them have lagoon  systems and so in those lagoon systems  it's really hard to get a sense of the  flow in and out of those lagoon systems  however in in when we're analyzing the  data we do take into account the flow  data and then that is also taken into  account when we use those normalizing  biomarkers  we also  have looked at potentially normalizing  to population but when we were looking  at lawrence again we tended to avoid  that for two reasons we had some pretty  large shifts in population  over the course of the pandemic and so  how did we account for that large shift  in population was really difficult  the other thing to be aware of is  depending on how you're collecting your  sample the number of people contributing  to a collection system varies so  for example  this is more anecdotal 